WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.233
Today, we're

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:00.834
getting new and

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:01.835
disturbing insights

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:03.770
into the mental state of Brian Coworker,

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:05.238
the PhD criminology

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.840
student accused of murdering

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.675
four University of Idaho students.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.010
The New York Times

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.277
has uncovered online

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.312
posts from his past

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:13.947
that paint a picture of an isolated

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.181
and depressed teenager.

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:17.283
And one Post coworker writes,

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.352
I can say and do whatever I want

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.688
with little remorse and another.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:22.989
As I hug my family,

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.190
I look into their faces.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.025
I see nothing.

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:27.494
It is like I am looking at a video game.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:28.895
Kabuga is currently being held

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.463
on four counts of first degree murder

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:31.031
in the fatal

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:33.733
stabbings of Kaley Gonsalves,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.602
Madison, Mogan,

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:39.039
Xana, Carreno, Condal and Ethan Chapman

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.740
joining us now is CNN chief

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:41.641
law enforcement analyst

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.044
John Miller and retired FBI profiler

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.211
Kathy Canning Melo.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.480
She's also an instructor of criminology

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.382
at the University of North Carolina.

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.550
At Wilmington.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.819
Kathy, let me start with you first.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:53.553
How typical is it

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:54.788
to learn that a suspected

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.656
killer viewed their own life,

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.559
as it were, a video game without emotion?

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.193
I mean, when you hear

00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.227
about something like that,

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.931
some of that starts to make sense.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:06.066
Yeah, absolutely.

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:08.468
I find these texts really fascinating

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:09.702
and they give us some really,

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.072
I think, important insights as to

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.373
what Brian

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.443
Berger was feeling during his teen years,

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:20.013
where he was experiencing a dark period.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:20.280
Right.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.048
We have him addicted to heroin

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:23.116
at the time.

00:01:23.116 --> 00:01:25.552
He's talking about suicidal ideation.

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:27.187
He's talking about

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:30.090
this lack of emotion that certainly

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.425
we see evidenced at the crime scene.

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:34.594
And John,

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.062
how do you think all of these details

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.464
will impact the case

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:38.431
as investigators

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.000
try to determine a motive?

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:41.701
Well, motive

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.104
has been still the burning question here,

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.406
because there is still no indication

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.308
that he knew any of the victims.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:49.976
And it is such

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:51.511
a personal and hateful crime.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.645
And yet,

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.848
you know, as Kathy could underscore,

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.816
when I mean every offender

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:58.017
in the serial killer

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.052
or mass murderer

00:01:59.052 --> 00:02:00.620
or field is going to be

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.155
highly individualized.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.256
Each one is different.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:04.424
That's a given.

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:06.092
And yet there are common traits

00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:07.694
that run across these offenders.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.796
And one of them,

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.432
and he kind of reads us through this

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:13.533
in these emails is

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:17.270
an inability to feel sympathy,

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:20.373
no relationship with empathy,

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.709
an inability to feel guilt or remorse,

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.979
which, of course, is is the combination,

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.180
the emotional cocktail

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.150
that allows them to do the things they do

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:32.852
and then to be able to repeat those acts

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.889
again if they're not stopped.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:36.789
And, John,

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:37.423
this detail

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:37.924
in the Times

00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:38.591
that the suspect

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:39.526
became fascinated

00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:40.860
with criminal psychology,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:41.861
said he hoped to provide

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:43.096
counseling for high,

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:44.564
high profile criminals.

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:45.298
I mean, that is

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:46.633
that's a wrinkle

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:47.400
that you don't see in

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.402
many of these types of cases.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:50.336
We do know, Jim.

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:50.770
Yeah,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.605
this is highly unusual,

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.274
but it's also in this case,

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:57.243
there are so many things that really beg

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:58.511
to be dug into more.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.480
If you look at the

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.681
the posting he did

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.249
as part of his master's project,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.217
asking criminals

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:05.084
to come in

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:06.286
and be interviewed

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.121
with him as the coordinator

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.490
about the psychological traits

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.692
of decision making during their crimes.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.093
What were you thinking

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.562
when you did this violent act?

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:17.463
How did it make you feel?

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:18.731
What was your plan?

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.267
What, if anything, did you leave behind?

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.001
On one hand,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:22.502
you could say

00:03:22.502 --> 00:03:23.603
that was a study,

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:24.537
although the structure

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:25.872
is very nonacademic.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:26.573
On the other hand,

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:30.343
you could say that this was as

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.746
as Mary Ellen O'Toole posited last night.

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:36.416
This was an act of voyeurism

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:37.650
meaning these interviews

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.520
would allow him to immerse himself in

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:41.688
and feelings

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:42.655
that he was having

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.224
by experiencing them through others

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.293
That is disturbing.

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:48.661
And Kathy,

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:48.962
The New York

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:50.863
Times says that one of co-worker's

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.732
friends told them

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:53.633
or told this individual

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.735
he suffer from a neurological condition

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:58.905
called visual snow, where

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.006
people see scattered

00:04:00.006 --> 00:04:02.075
dots like static on a TV.

00:04:02.108 --> 00:04:03.209
And one of the posts,

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.844
as I'm reading here,

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.547
it is as if the ringing in my ears

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:08.581
and the fuzz in my vision

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.550
is simply all the demons in my head

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:11.851
mocking me.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:13.987
Doesn't sound good.

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.956
No, it sounds very disturbing, but

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.758
we're going to have to have,

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:20.059
I think, more

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:23.663
deep interviews of people who know Brian

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.164
well in order

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.834
to understand that condition that he had

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:28.968
and any impact

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.803
it might have had on his

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:34.474
behavior at the time or later in life,

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:36.309
if the condition still exists,

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.710
we don't know that.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.412
So I think it's going to be incumbent

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:41.948
upon prosecutors, investigators.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:42.448
It is great

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:43.750
now that they have a few more months

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.917
to do this.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.220
To to conduct these long,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.355
behaviorally oriented interviews.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:50.590
When I was in The View,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.591
we had an instrument called

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:52.625
the General Assessment

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:54.360
Questionnaire that had a hundred

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.728
questions

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.564
that probed into areas of an individual's

00:04:58.564 --> 00:04:59.432
life, their

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.900
their personality traits,

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:05.505
their hobbies, their attitudes and it was

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:07.473
meaning to

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:10.376
get more insight about their behaviors,

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.811
because we have some, you know,

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:13.713
casual contacts that said, you know,

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:14.514
he's charming.

00:05:14.514 --> 00:05:16.516
Well, do we really know he's charming?

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:18.985
Does he lack empathy?

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.287
Is he Cunningham manipulative?

00:05:22.322 --> 00:05:23.856
Is he controlling

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:26.693
Does he harbor misogynistic feelings?

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.594
All of these things

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.063
are going to be evident

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:31.898
in people that know him well.

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.033
Certainly we're getting some indications

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:35.535
of his personality

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:37.437
through his online persona,

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:38.838
but we need to understand him

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:39.772
offline as well,

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:40.573
because at some point,

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:42.175
the prosecutor is going to have to paint

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:43.109
a picture

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.944
of this individual to a jury

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:45.912
and they're going to have to make

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:47.780
some connections in their head.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.415
Is this the kind of person

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.050
no one physically capable?

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.051
But number two,

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:54.420
psychologically, emotionally, capable

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:57.023
of committing such heinous crimes?

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:58.391
And John Cobourg

00:05:58.391 --> 00:05:59.625
has maintained his innocence

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:01.027
through his lawyer.

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.228
But I mean, given

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:04.497
what we've discussed so far,

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:07.033
there is a decent amount of evidence

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:08.835
that police have that investigators

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:10.169
have in this case, do they not?

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:11.938
That is true.

00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:13.406
And I mean, we're going by

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:14.540
the American standard,

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.942
which is he is innocent

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:17.310
until proven guilty.

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.144
But when you look

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.012
at the probable cause document,

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.748
you can certainly see,

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:22.749
based on the evidence

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:24.016
that they've put forward,

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:25.651
why he's charged in this case.

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.154
And you can certainly see

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:29.555
and this is something

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:30.957
that people really haven't focused

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:33.393
on, is authorities in Pennsylvania

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:34.961
that have put together the arrest

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:35.895
warrant and execute

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.696
the arrest

00:06:36.696 --> 00:06:39.332
are now going very carefully backwards

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:41.501
through cold Berger's entire history.

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:42.101
From the time

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:43.169
those emails were written

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:45.405
as an adolescent, right up to the time

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:46.539
he left to

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:47.640
to go to the Washington,

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.408
Idaho area,

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:49.442
because

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:51.377
there is an overarching theory

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:54.747
that a multiple murder of four people

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:55.681
in the middle of the night

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.516
is probably

00:06:56.516 --> 00:06:59.552
not an offender's first act of violence.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:01.421
They're looking at unsolved cases.

00:07:01.421 --> 00:07:03.489
They're going to use that DNA

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:05.792
to compare them to samples they have.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.593
They're looking for

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:09.529
any stalking behavior.

00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:10.830
They're really kind of

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.331
going backwards to say,

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:13.900
was there something we missed here?

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:15.401
Right.

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:16.369
Especially when you

00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:16.769
go through

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.137
some of the things that he said

00:07:18.137 --> 00:07:18.905
about himself.

00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:21.073
I mean, that is very EYE-OPENING stuff.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:21.274
All right.

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:22.475
John Miller,

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:23.876
Kathy Canning Melo,

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:24.777
thanks for that discussion.

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:26.512
Fascinating discussion.

